<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements. Notarization REQUIRES hardened runtime, and hardened
  runtime kills JIT-compiling processes unless they are entitled for it. StarNet ships two:
  the Tauri shell (WKWebView) and the bundled Node sidecar (V8). Without these the app
  codesigns and notarizes fine and then dies on launch — the failure lands at runtime, not
  at build time, so it is easy to ship broken.

  ⛔ CHANGED 2026-07-30: THE SIDECAR IS NO LONGER PURE JS. The bundle now carries the offline
  voice engine (onnxruntime-node), which loads third-party native `.node` binaries we do not
  sign. Under hardened runtime, library validation refuses to load exactly that, so
  `disable-library-validation` became REQUIRED — the app would otherwise notarize cleanly and
  then fail the moment Local Live warms a model. The previous note here asserted "zero native
  .node addons (verified 2026-07-24)" and said to add this key only if that ever changed; this
  is that change, recorded rather than quietly flipped.

  What it costs: the process may load native code signed by someone other than us. That is a
  real reduction in guarantee, accepted because the alternative is shipping an offline voice
  feature that cannot start on macOS. If the bundled engine is ever removed, REMOVE THIS KEY
  with it — an entitlement kept "just in case" is a permanent weakening for a temporary reason.

  Deliberately still NOT included:
    App Sandbox keys — Developer ID (non-App-Store) distribution is not sandboxed, so the
      sidecar's 127.0.0.1 listener and outbound HTTPS need no network entitlement.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- WKWebView microphone capture also needs hardened-runtime resource access.
       NSMicrophoneUsageDescription alone cannot authorize a signed desktop build. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- V8 and WKWebView both generate and execute code at runtime. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Node's V8 maps executable pages it wrote itself; allow-jit alone does not always
       cover that path. Both are what Electron/Node-embedding apps ship. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- onnxruntime-node's native binaries are third-party and unsigned by us; hardened runtime
       will not dlopen them without this. Required by the bundled offline voice engine. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
